module Numerics

# Suflae's surface of the numbers it shares with RazorForge: the same values, with `Integer` in place of the
# fixed-width integers that give a precision, a number of places, an exponent or a radix. A Suflae program
# calls these in place of the RazorForge routines called the same way.

### Parses an integer from text using the requested radix (2 to 64).
### The input is an optional leading `-` followed by digits of the selected base (letters are
### case-insensitive up to base 36). A trailing `\r` or `\n` ends the digits; empty digits read as 0.
### :param text: The textual integer representation.
### :param radix: The numeric base to use.
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
### :throws: {ValueError} if `text` is not a valid integer in `radix`.
failable routine Integer(text: Text, radix: Integer = 10) -> Integer
    return Integer(text: text, radix: radix.to[S64]().to[S32]())

### Shifts this value left by `bits` positions (`me << bits`).
### :param bits: The number of bit positions to shift, 0 or more (below 0 crashes).
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
routine Integer.shift_left(bits: Integer) -> Integer
    return me.shift_left(bits: bits.to_shift_amount())

### Shifts this value right by `bits` positions (`me >> bits`): `floor(me / 2^bits)`, so `-5 >> 1` is `-3`.
### :param bits: The number of bit positions to shift, 0 or more (below 0 crashes).
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
routine Integer.arith_shift_right(bits: Integer) -> Integer
    return me.arith_shift_right(bits: bits.to_shift_amount())

### Shifts this value right by `bits` positions filling with zeros (`me >>> bits`). An `Integer` has no fixed
### width, so a negative value has no zero-filled form: this is `>>` for a value that is not negative.
### :param bits: The number of bit positions to shift, 0 or more (below 0 crashes).
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
### :throws: {NumericDomainError} when this value is negative.
failable routine Integer.logical_shift_right(bits: Integer) -> Integer
    return me.logical_shift_right(bits: bits.to_shift_amount())

### Raises this value to a non-negative integer exponent.
### :param exp: The exponent value.
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
### :throws: {NumericDomainError} when `exp` is negative (the result would not be an integer).
failable routine Integer.pow(exp: Integer) -> Integer
    if exp < 0
        throw NumericDomainError(description: "negative exponent: no integer result")
    return me.pow(exp: exp.to[U64]().to[U32]())

### Returns the sign of this value as `-1`, `0`, or `1`.
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
routine Integer.signum() -> Integer
    return me.signum().to[Integer]()

### Returns the number of bits in the magnitude of this value (0 for zero).
### :returns: The resulting `Integer` value.
routine Integer.bit_length() -> Integer
    return me.bit_length().to[Integer]()

### Formats this value as text in the requested radix (2 to 64; digits past 9 are uppercase
### letters up to base 36). An out-of-range radix renders an empty text.
### :param radix: The numeric base to use.
### :returns: The resulting `Text` value.
routine Integer.represent_radix(radix: Integer) -> Text
    return me.represent_radix(radix: radix.to[S64]().to[S32]())

### Renders this value in base `radix` (2 to 36), digits past 9 as lowercase letters, with a leading `-`
### when negative. Crashes on any other radix (unlike `represent_radix`, which renders an empty text).
### :param radix: The numeric base.
### :returns: The rendered `Text`.
routine Integer.to_text(radix: Integer) -> Text
    return me.to_text(radix: radix.to[S64]().to[S32]())
